{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q008_v1_atp_synthase_bulge_classification","answer":"Verified in SCOPe 2.08: the V1 ATP synthase A subunit bulge domain is its own superfamily, b.84.5 (sunid 310577, "V1 ATP synthase A subunit, bulge domain-like"), with one family b.84.5.1 (sunid 310616). Its proteins are V1 (sunid 310706) and A1 (sunid 310707) ATP synthase A subunit bulge domains, e.g. d3a5ca3 (3a5c A:113-184, T. thermophilus), d3j9ta3 (yeast) and d1vdza2/d3ikja2 (P. horikoshii). It sits in the fold b.84 "Barrel-sandwich hybrid" (sunid 51229), class b (All beta proteins, sunid 48724). The sibling superfamilies in that fold are b.84.1 Single hybrid motif, b.84.2 Rudiment single hybrid motif, b.84.3 Duplicated hybrid motif and b.84.4 Ribosomal L27 protein-like. The domain shares the fold with them but has its own superfamily node.

Interpretation: the placement makes two claims at different strengths. (1) Fold level: the bulge domain has the barrel-sandwich-hybrid architecture, so it is structurally similar to the other b.84 members. Shared fold alone does not establish common ancestry. (2) Superfamily level: the bulge-domain members (V1 and A1 A subunits, across bacteria, archaea and eukaryotes) are claimed to be homologous, i.e. to share probable common ancestry. Being a separate superfamily means SCOPe makes no positive homology assertion linking them to the hybrid-motif superfamilies b.84.1-b.84.4. The likely reason is that the sequence, structural and functional evidence did not support merging them. The API does not expose the curators' rationale, alignment or literature argument. The only annotation is a PubMed reference, 19893485, on the superfamily, and I did not read it. So the exact reason for the split is not verified here.

Limits: separate superfamilies do not prove the bulge domain is unrelated, analogous or convergent. They mean only that no homology is asserted at that level, and the question is left open. The homology endpoint for b.84.5 returns no warnings, heterogeneity or repeat flags, but that absence is not positive evidence. Superfamily placement does not transfer specific function, catalytic role or a usable structural template from the hybrid-motif proteins, such as biotinyl/lipoyl carriers or peptidase-M23 type domains. It also does not fix a mechanism or ligand role. The d3a5ca3 comment "complexed with adp" is only a ligand note on that entry. I did not check the sibling superfamilies' membership or lineages beyond the child list of b.84, and I did not retrieve alignments or topology, so I make no claim about how close the bulge domain is structurally to any specific sibling.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=bulge&limit=20","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310577","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310577/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310577/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310577/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51229/children?limit=100","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3a5ca3/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3a5ca3/annotations"]}]}
